# Every filed layer as the rules reach it. One row per filed layer: how many rules examine it, how
# many found a violation, and whether anything reaches it at all.
#
# `rank/incidence_reach.sqlc`, read the other way
#   Read this way, the shortfall has names. That relation counts, per relation, how many layers it
#   touches; it is a count per relation, so it can say how many and never which, and a layer nothing
#   reaches leaves no row anywhere to be counted. This is the same measurement read the other way,
#   where the subject is the layer. It is the third of the reach relations, after `incidence_reach`
#   and `guard_reach`.
#
# It needs a key that resolves
#   It depends on the second arm of `checks/grain.sqlc`. A rule that puts a claim address in the
#   contract's `layer` column names no layer, and this relation, joined on that key, drops those
#   pairs and prints a believable table that reports layers as unreachable when they are not: a
#   by-product of the mis-declared subject rather than a fact about the model. Coverage measured
#   through a key that does not resolve measures the key.
#
# Depth, not a yes or no
#   The useful reading is depth. On this corpus every filed layer is reached by at least one rule,
#   which makes `nothing_reaches_it` a guard that can fire rather than a finding. What varies is how
#   much: a layer one rule can speak about is a layer one repair silences, and that shows here and
#   nowhere else.
#
# `examined_by` counts rules, not rows
#   On purpose. A rule at the claim's grain returns many rows for one layer, and counting those
#   would grade a layer by how many claims it happens to file rather than by how many different
#   questions this repository can put to it.
#
# A rule that examined nothing does not reach this layer
#   `checks/all.sqlc` joins its roster first, so a rule with an empty population still returns a
#   row, with `violates` NULL and no filing. Those rows carry no layer, so they cannot arrive here,
#   which keeps the vacuous verdict where it belongs: a fact about the rule, not about the layer.
-- checks/all.sqlc counted per layer, against every layer in layers/every_layer.sqlc.
SELECT l.filing,
       l.layer,
       count(DISTINCT c.rule)                                   AS examined_by,
       count(DISTINCT c.rule) FILTER (WHERE c.violates)         AS violated,
       count(DISTINCT c.rule) = 0                               AS nothing_reaches_it
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/every_layer.sqlc)
) l
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(checks/all.sqlc)
) c ON c.filing = l.filing AND c.layer = l.layer
GROUP BY l.filing, l.layer
